    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d713">

                <pb facs="0834" n="1668" xml:id="pg1668"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 652.119/566a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Willard</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-02-19T21:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 19, 1918, 9 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>896. Referring your Nos. 1058 and 1073.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d713-f1"/> There are
                    no Spanish steamers held. <hi rendition="#italic">Adolfo, Jacinto Verdaguer,
                        Miguel Jover, Andalucia, Cataluña</hi> are awaiting licenses for portions of
                    cargo such as cotton. Export licenses will be granted immediately upon Spain’s
                    signing the agreements with the French and United States, their cargoes to count
                    as a part of the monthly quotas provided for in article 4. This would permit all
                    Spanish vessels to proceed immediately upon signing agreements. You may give
                    assurances to the above effect in whatever manner is necessary to help prompt
                    closing of agreement. During two months from date of signing of the agreement no
                    detention of vessels will be enforced whose cargoes do not involve a violation
                    of the terms of the agreement or unless vessels have violated any of the
                    regulations imposed by United States authorities on all neutral tonnage clearing
                    from American ports. You may rest assured there is no discrimination against
                    Spanish tonnage pending definite agreement to be arrived at as provided in
                    article 13.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
